    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#robert-guiscard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard.jpg" alt="Robert Guiscard" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Guiscard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom grew out of the Norman conquest of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>. In the eleventh century Norman adventurers led by the Hauteville family took Apulia and Calabria from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> princes, and between 1061 and 1091 Count Roger I conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a> from its Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emir">emirs</a>, taking Palermo in 1072 together with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a>. Roger I’s son Roger II, count of Sicily from 1105, united the Norman south when he secured the Duchy of Apulia and Calabria after the death of his cousin Duke William in 1127. During the papal schism of 1130 he supported Antipope Anacletus II, who granted him a royal title, and Roger II was crowned in Palermo Cathedral on Christmas Day 1130. The new kingdom had to be defended by force: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">Pope</a> Innocent II, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lothair-i">Lothair</a> III and rebellious mainland barons and cities fought Roger for most of the 1130s. Roger subdued Capua, Bari and Naples, and in 1139 his son captured Innocent II at Galluccio; by the Treaty of Mignano the pope then recognised Roger as king of Sicily, duke of Apulia and prince of Capua. The kingdom thus joined the island to all of southern Italy as far north as the Abruzzi, forming one of the richest and most centralised states of twelfth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-ii-of-aragon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Ferdinand II of Aragon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michel_Sittow_004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Michael Sittow, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a> inherited a mixed population of Greek Christians, Arabic-speaking Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jews">Jews</a>, to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> were added. Muslim farmers, Greek monks and Latin knights lived side by side, and the court used Greek, Arabic and Latin. The island was fertile and exported grain, while Palermo, Messina and the Apulian ports traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-africa">North Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#levant">Levant</a> and the Italian maritime cities; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> working, introduced in Byzantine and Islamic times, was reinforced when Greek silk workers were carried off from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thebes">Thebes</a> and Corinth in 1147. Over the twelfth and thirteenth centuries Latin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a> and feudal lordship gradually displaced the Muslim communities, and after revolts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> deported the remaining Muslims of Sicily to Lucera in Apulia in the 1220s. After 1282 the island kingdom’s economy rested on grain exported by Genoese, Catalan and other merchants, on large estates (<em>latifondi</em>) owned by barons and the Church, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, silk and tuna fisheries. The population was largely Catholic, though Greek rite communities persisted, and the Jews, an important part of urban life, were expelled in 1492–1493 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a>. The Val di Noto earthquake of 1693 destroyed much of south-east Sicily, which was rebuilt in the Baroque style that still marks Noto, Ragusa and Catania.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#charles-i-of-anjou"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou.jpg" alt="Charles I of Anjou" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Some_Old_Guy_2_(5642308121)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Son of Groucho from Scotland, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roger II combined Norman knights, Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#archery">archers</a> and a powerful fleet. Under his admiral George of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antioch">Antioch</a> Sicilian fleets captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tripoli">Tripoli</a> in 1146 and Mahdia in 1148, creating a short-lived dominion on the North African coast known as the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> overran by 1160, and in 1147 raided Corfu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thebes">Thebes</a> and Corinth. William II sent expeditions against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 1174, which failed, and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 1185, which sacked Thessaloniki before being defeated. Henry VI of Hohenstaufen conquered the kingdom in 1194, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> defended it against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a>, using Muslim soldiers from Lucera. Manfred, Frederick’s son, was killed at the Battle of Benevento in 1266 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, who then defeated Conradin at Tagliacozzo in 1268. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a>, which broke out in Palermo in March 1282, began twenty years of war between the Angevins of Naples and the Aragonese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a>, the Italian-born admiral of the Aragonese fleet, won repeated naval victories; the Peace of Caltabellotta of 1302 left the island to Frederick III. Under Spanish rule Sicily was a bulwark against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, supplying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galley">galleys</a> and grain for the relief of Malta in 1565 and the Lepanto campaign of 1571. Messina’s revolt of 1674–1678 brought a French fleet, and in 1718 a British fleet destroyed the Spanish navy off Cape Passaro.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#emir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emir.jpg" alt="Emir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#emir">Emir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuja_Shah_Durrani_and_Men_in_his_decorated_palace_Wellcome_V0050531.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Norman kings built an unusually centralised state. They kept Arab and Byzantine fiscal practices and created financial offices known as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#diwan">diwan</a></em> to record lands and revenues, while the royal <em>curia</em> appointed justiciars and chamberlains to administer the provinces. Roger II issued the Assizes of Ariano, traditionally dated to 1140, and ministers such as George of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antioch">Antioch</a> and Maio of Bari held the Greek and Arabic title of emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emir">emirs</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> the Constitutions of Melfi of 1231 set out a comprehensive law code that limited baronial and church jurisdiction, and in 1224 he founded the University of Naples to train officials. After the Vespers the island kingdom developed a parliament with three chambers, or <em>bracci</em>, of barons, clergy and royal towns, whose consent was needed for taxes. Under the cadet Aragonese line, especially in the fourteenth century, royal power weakened and great baronial families such as the Chiaramonte and Ventimiglia dominated. From 1412 the kingdom belonged to the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and later to the Spanish monarchy, ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> in Palermo, with Spanish councils and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a>, introduced in 1487, but it kept its own laws, parliament and privileges. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king Ferdinand, sheltering in Palermo under British protection, accepted a liberal constitution in 1812 that formally abolished feudalism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Charles_V,_Holy_Roman_Emperor,_seated_(1500%E2%80%931558),_formerly_attributed_to_Titian_(Alte_Pinakothek,_Munich).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Norman kingdom is famous for a court culture that drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a>, Greek and Arabic traditions. Its monuments, among them the Palatine Chapel with its Byzantine mosaics and Islamic painted ceiling, Cefalù Cathedral and the cathedral of Monreale begun by William II in 1174, are now inscribed together as a World Heritage Site. The Arab geographer al-Idrisi completed his great world geography, the <em>Book of Roger</em>, for Roger II in 1154, and translations of Greek and Arabic works into Latin were made at the court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, known as <em>stupor mundi</em>, wrote a treatise on falconry and patronised the Sicilian School of poets, whose love lyrics in a Sicilian vernacular produced the sonnet, usually credited to Giacomo da Lentini, and influenced Dante and Petrarch. The Constitutions of Melfi were one of the most ambitious legal codes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The Vespers of 1282 became a symbol of popular resistance and inspired Giuseppe Verdi’s opera, and the division they caused gave rise to two kingdoms both called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a> and eventually to the name Two Sicilies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> granted Malta, part of the kingdom, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> in 1530. Sicily’s mixed heritage, from Arab-derived place names to Spanish-era Baroque, continues to shape its regional identity within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-utrecht"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht.jpg" alt="Treaty of Utrecht" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Treaty_of_Utrecht_(clean).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The_Treaty_of_Utrecht.jpg: Original uploader was RedCoat10 at en.wikipedia derivative work: Angel paez (talk), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom’s decline began with the war over the Norman inheritance and the Hohenstaufen–papal conflict, but the decisive break was the Vespers of 1282. The mainland stayed with the Angevins as the Kingdom of Naples, while the island, under the Aragonese, became a smaller realm whose independent kings ruled until the death of Martin the Younger in 1409. After the Compromise of Caspe in 1412 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a> was governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> as a province of the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and, from 1516, of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. Under Spanish rule it remained loyal but restless: revolts broke out in Palermo in 1647 and Messina in 1674, and taxes, baronial power and grain shortages weighed on the population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713 gave Sicily to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> of Savoy, who exchanged it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardinia">Sardinia</a> with the Austrian Habsburgs in 1720. In 1734–1735 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-iii-of-spain">Charles of Bourbon</a> conquered Naples and Sicily and was crowned in Palermo, uniting the two crowns in one person but keeping separate kingdoms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand III of Sicily</a>, who as Ferdinand IV also ruled Naples, twice took refuge on the island during the French wars. After his restoration in Naples he merged the two realms by laws of December 1816 into the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies, abolishing the Sicilian constitution and parliament and ending the separate kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a> created under Roger II one of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>’s most centralised and cosmopolitan states, blending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a>, Greek and Arabic government and art, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a> of 1282 survived for five more centuries as an island kingdom under Aragonese, Spanish, Savoyard, Austrian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> rulers.</em></p>

